{
 "id": "L2",
 "n": 2,
 "title": "People",
 "goal": "Introduce yourself and your family, say where you are from and what you do, and ask the same of others.",
 "notes": [
  {
   "h": "I and my",
   "p": "With people you don't know well, 'I' is {{저}} and 'my' is {{제}}: {{저는 의사예요}} (I'm a doctor), {{제 이름}} (my name), {{제 아내}} (my wife). The casual words {{나}} and {{내}} are for close friends, so leave them for now.",
   "ex": [
    [
     "저는 의사예요",
     "I'm a doctor."
    ],
    [
     "제 이름은 댄이에요",
     "My name is Dan."
    ],
    [
     "제 아내예요",
     "This is my wife."
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "Am, is, are, and is not",
   "p": "Add {{이에요}} or {{예요}} to a noun to say 'it is / I am'. After a final consonant use {{이에요}} ({{사람이에요}}); after a vowel use {{예요}} ({{의사예요}}). For 'is not', the noun takes {{이}} or {{가}} and then {{아니에요}}: {{학생이 아니에요}}, {{의사가 아니에요}}. Same rule: {{이}} after a consonant, {{가}} after a vowel.",
   "ex": [
    [
     "저는 미국 사람이에요",
     "I'm American."
    ],
    [
     "저는 의사예요",
     "I'm a doctor."
    ],
    [
     "저는 학생이 아니에요",
     "I'm not a student."
    ],
    [
     "저는 의사가 아니에요",
     "I'm not a doctor."
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "The topic marker",
   "p": "{{은}} or {{는}} after a word marks what you are talking about, like 'as for …': {{저는}} (as for me), {{제 이름은}} (as for my name). Use {{은}} after a consonant and {{는}} after a vowel. Asking someone's name uses {{이}} instead: {{이름이 뭐예요?}}",
   "ex": [
    [
     "저는 미국 사람이에요",
     "I'm American."
    ],
    [
     "제 이름은 댄이에요",
     "My name is Dan."
    ],
    [
     "제 친구는 선생님이에요",
     "My friend is a teacher."
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "This, that, and that over there",
   "p": "Korean has three pointing words. {{이}} = this, near me. {{그}} = that, near you, or the one we just mentioned. {{저}} = that over there, far from both of us. Put them before a noun ({{이 사람}}, {{저 사람}}) or use the 'thing' forms {{이거}}, {{그거}}, {{저거}}. Ask about things with {{뭐예요?}} (what is it?) and about people with {{누구예요?}} (who is it?). Careful: {{저}} also means 'I'; the context tells you which.",
   "ex": [
    [
     "이거 뭐예요?",
     "What is this?"
    ],
    [
     "그거 뭐예요?",
     "What is that (near you)?"
    ],
    [
     "저 사람은 누구예요?",
     "Who is that person over there?"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "Sounds: linking and tensing",
   "p": "When a syllable ends in a consonant and the next one starts with a vowel, the consonant slides over: {{사람이에요}} sounds like {{사라미에요}}, and {{이름은}} like {{이르믄}}. After a final ㄱ sound, a following ㅅ is said tense, like ㅆ: {{한국 사람}} sounds like {{한국 싸람}}. Watch the 'said like' line.",
   "ex": [
    [
     "저는 미국 사람이에요",
     "I'm American."
    ],
    [
     "제 이름은 댄이에요",
     "My name is Dan."
    ],
    [
     "한국 사람",
     "a Korean"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ],
